{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 898 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b8b7bb21db2e456fcae083a21752a4fa10beda2b2db78034c091798e731812eb" :edb/db-before-t 897 :edb/db-after-t 898 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534210 50 #inst "2026-10-06T11:09:42.065Z" 13194139534210 true] [17592186175689 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIs the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$ on the intervals $[-1,2]$ and $[-1,0]?$" 13194139534210 true] [17592186175689 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n${\\frac{1}{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ne 1 \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x = 1$.\nIs the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$ on the intervals $[-1,2]$ and $[-1,0]$?" 13194139534210 false] [17592186175689 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,2]$. We have\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})f(x)|= lim_(x → 1^{+})\\frac{1}{x - 1} = + ∞$,\nso $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$. Therefore, $f(x)$ is *not* continuous on the interval $[-1,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[-1,0]$. The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ is at $x=1$, which is not in the interval $[-1,0]$. Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-1,0]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, of the two intervals, the extreme value theorem is only applicable on $[-1,0]$." 13194139534210 false] [17592186175689 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-1}=+\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is *not* continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider $[-1,0].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval $[-1,0].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-1,0],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, of the two intervals, the extreme value theorem is only applicable on $[-1,0].$" 13194139534210 true] [17592186175690 1100 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981801/assets/66cd2a8b7e88849efc1b8275fd35f763984e4444821a9cd0e0e9060a8f90c956.png)\n\n    \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-2,1]$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $[-4,4]$" 13194139534210 true] [17592186175690 1100 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3571/Source/Images/3b8ef5690fbe8b89c561afb3ee490431.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$?\n\n1. $[-2,1]$\n2. $(-2,1)$\n3. $[-4,4]$" 13194139534210 false] [17592186175690 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-2,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,1]$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n- The interval $(-2,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n- The interval $[-4,4]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[-4,4]$ because there are discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=2$, both of which lie within $[-4,4]$. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the 