-1,0)$" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282538 1113 #uuid "f97a27ef-55cd-5290-b60b-237687becd4e" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282538 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-1,1].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now consider  $[0.5,1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[0.5,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[0.5,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we note that the interval $[-1,0)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282540 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282541 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282542 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282543 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282544 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1041 "selection" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282544 13194139534210 true] [17592186282539 1110 #uuid "86e30657-ebc5-5d23-8b46-37c71a515a4b" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282540 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282540 1111 #uuid "ac9b5207-e12f-55a8-b522-ea9e1363c935" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282541 1034 "I and