#uuid "eb7702c1-237e-53f3-aa89-942da52cc8d9" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282597 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282597 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282597 1111 #uuid "629d753a-dfff-515b-b5c3-5d359a1718bb" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282598 1034 "I only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282598 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282598 1111 #uuid "53bb38f6-926a-5169-a3bd-89b0521fb34c" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282599 1034 "III only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282599 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282599 1111 #uuid "93505524-6f17-5003-a079-677467d8f77c" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282600 1034 "II only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282600 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282600 1111 #uuid "7ae011de-ebfb-5dd9-8698-328b5b89c35d" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282602 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1089 "q-73504" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1100 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[2,\\pi ]$" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1113 #uuid "fc352233-846d-594d-b612-bda9c3885224" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282601 1145 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282603 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282604 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282605 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282606 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282607 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1041 "selection" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282605 13194139534210 true] [17592186282602 1110 #uuid "c930ffaa-1e59-54a6-b426-7c5fa6e87ff2" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282603 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282603 1111 #uuid "5d14eb47-4056-5aa8-bb4e-034503b19308" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282604 1034 "I only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282604 1111 #uuid "26760daf-00bb-5e81-a1ed-c39ebbea0601" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282605 1034 "III only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282605 1111 #uuid "395dd8bf-24c7-5dd0-85bc-9c7a9dce0816" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282606 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534210 true] [17592186282606 1111 #uuid "c3ff5d18-e1fd-5831-a5bc-b2e886054b8e" 13194139534210 true] [17592186282607 1034 "II  o